III.Program Description (3 Page Limit):
A.Detailed Description (Addressing the goals in section 1.4): The project is designed to provide early
intervention in-home services that will increase the capacity of parents and children, thereby, providing
them with tools to maintain children safely at home.The program is also designed to help families
access services, assess the needs of the participants, and to help families develop action plans to
address those needs..
B.Program Components:
-1.Need:.Stressful times, create stressed families.whichmay.result in more incidence of delinquency,
substance abuse, family dysfunction, abuse and neglect, etc..These risk factors increase the number
of out of home placements.This is particularly true of the low-income children and single-parent
families who may have less resources available to them. In 2011, 59% of the children referred to the
program were from severely low-income families and an additional 17 % were from low to moderate
income families. In the past ten years numerous local and statewide needs assessments have
confirmed the importance of early intervention services.
2.Key Components:
a.Assessments: An individual and family assessment on families referred to the program will be
conducted to determine what services are needed.
b.Youth Mentoring: Research has shown that an enduring and supportive mentoring relationship
can powerfully influence the quality of a child's life
c.In-Home Parent Mentors: Adult mentors will be recruited, screened and trained to provide
in-home parenting support to parents.
C.Existing data demonstrating the program's success.(See Section G below)
1.Sustainability: Youth Alternatives (YA) has been a department of the City of Cheyenne for over
United Way and the Department of Family Services for over 25 years.This level of continued
support speaks to the programs credibility within the community.
2. Preferred Agency: In an independent survey conducted in 2012 Youth Alternatives was the
preferred agency among youth service providers for referrals for children with behavior and family
problems.
3. Comparison Study: In 2008 a study of parents of mentored and non-mentored youth in the Youth
Alternatives Program indicated that mentored showed significant improvement in the areas of
school performance, relationships and self-confidence.
receiving services through Youth Alternatives at thelower court level were convicted of a crime at
the higher court level.
5.Improved School Performance: In 2008 the Wyoming Survey and Analysis Center(WYSAC)
indicated young people in the after-school academic enrichment program showed repeated school
improvements over5years."
6.Helping Kids Graduate and Preventing Delinquency: In 2012 a review of 200
