3.Research:(Continued from section C above)
Numerous studies concluded mentoring produced a range of benefits that increased the
resiliency of children and families.This included studies by the Search Institute(Assets Study,
1999), Grossman and Tierney (Does Mentoring Work,1998); Child Trends (Mentoring
Research Project,2002)and the National Mentoring Center (2000).
H.The specific geographic area to be served: Laramie County is the geographical area to be
served.
1.Community Partnerships: Youth Alternatives has a history of collaboration and has a strong
group of primary partners that it collaborates with to effectively implement prevention programs
for children and parents.
J.When the Services Will Be Provided: The majority of the programing will occur evenings and
weekends. On-call support staff will be available 24 hours a day.
K.Performance Measures:
1.Assessments: A minimum of 95% of program participants will complete a needs assessment,
A prevention plan will be developed from the assessment which will assist in providing
participants with appropriate services.
2.In-Home and Group Parenting Support: A minimum of 75% of program participants will
indicate improved parenting skills through pre and post-test comparisons.
3.Mentoring: A minimum of 75% of the program participants will report improvement in target
areas (i.e.self-concept, relationships, behavior, etc.)
4.Prevention and Recidivism: A minimum of 90% of the participants will not be placed in
out-of home placement and a minimum of 75%of participants will not return to placement.
5.Under-served Families: A minimum of 75% of under-served families will report a greater
awareness of services and improvement in the targeted areas listed under Mentoring above.
L.Replication: Youth Alternatives will continue to provide materials and consultation to any
communities that our wishing to duplicate the program.
M.Evaluation: The evaluation of the program will be based an analysis of the program
measures listed in Section K above
N.Tracking: Youth Alternatives will continue tracking program participants for a minimum of one
year after they have completed their involvement with the agency. The agency has previously
completed tracking studies and will utilize a similar process in the future
